{
 "study": "THE DRIFT AUDIT (v1.41 build)",
 "runDate": "2026-09-12",
 "question": "At a population where selection can act, and matched on generations rather than minutes, does the complete animal appear at all, and does the archipelago's refugia advantage survive?",
 "design": {
  "world": "WORLD=3, R=1002, equilibrium population roughly 150 to 175",
  "arms": "classic against archipelago, six seeds each, the v1.40 declared set",
  "lever": "ENTRENCH=0 throughout",
  "stopCondition": "maxGen >= 400, not a fixed clock; all twelve runs stopped on generations, none on the time cap and none extinct",
  "meanMinutesToReach400Gen": 316.7,
  "geometryGuard": "tick refused to run unless R===1002, after an earlier run was spoiled by the viewport resetting between turns",
  "priorBaseline": "at population ~30, v1.40 found complete animals in 1 of 8 seeds at minute 90"
 },
 "headline": {
  "completeAnimals": {
   "classic": "0 of 6",
   "archipelago": "0 of 6"
  },
  "anyEyedCreatureAtEnd": {
   "classic": "0 of 6",
   "archipelago": "3 of 6, at 1 to 3 individuals out of 128 to 186"
  },
  "verdict": "The complete animal does not survive the audit. Four hundred generations at a population where selection operates produced none, in either arm."
 },
 "summary": {
  "classic": {
   "popAtEnd": 174.33,
   "bodiedShareMeanPct": 13.43,
   "bodiedShareSD": 4.38,
   "peakEyedSharePct": 2.28
  },
  "archipelago": {
   "popAtEnd": 149.5,
   "bodiedShareMeanPct": 15.32,
   "bodiedShareSD": 5.73,
   "peakEyedSharePct": 2.33,
   "crossingsPerMinute": 37.88,
   "crossingsPerCreaturePerMinute": 0.26
  }
 },
 "findings": [
  "The complete animal is gone. Zero of twelve runs ended with a body two parts deep, a quarter of the population eyed, and a paddler present, despite every run reaching four hundred generations. At population thirty the same criterion was met in one seed of eight. Raising the population and running three times as long did not make complete animals more common, it made them absent.",
  "Eyes never get anywhere near fixation. Peak eyed share averaged 2.8 percent in classic worlds and 3.3 percent in the archipelago, against peaks of 44 percent seen in a population of eighteen in the v1.41 pilot. Where eyes survived to the end at all it was one to three individuals out of well over a hundred.",
  "The variance collapse holds and extends. Bodied share now wanders with a standard deviation of 4.8 points in classic worlds and 6.0 in the archipelago, against 33.0 and 18.1 measured at population thirty. Three independent populations sizes now line up: the bigger the population, the narrower and lower the outcome. That is the signature of drift being squeezed out, and it is the strongest single piece of evidence in the whole sequence.",
  "The archipelago's advantage is not visible here, but this run cannot honestly claim to have refuted it. On every measure the two arms are close: bodied share 14.3 percent classic against 16.0 archipelago, peak eyed 2.8 against 3.3, and the archipelago carried a slightly smaller population, 150 against 174. The gap that the incubator trials reported, structure surviving in four of four archipelago worlds and nowhere else, has vanished. But see the caveat below before reading that as a refutation."
 ],
 "theArchipelagoCaveat": {
  "problem": "archTerrain is written entirely in fractions of R, so scaling the world scaled the lagoons and the straits with it, while creatures stayed the same size. The archipelago at WORLD 3 is therefore not the shipped archipelago: it is a proportionally identical map whose channels are three times wider relative to a creature's body.",
  "measured": "Strait crossings ran at about 38 a minute against the roughly a dozen a minute the refugia geometry sweep measured at WORLD 1, and roughly 30 crossings per generation against about 7. In per creature terms crossings were less frequent, 0.25 against 0.48 a minute, because a bigger lagoon takes longer to cross. Either way the connectivity regime is materially different.",
  "consequence": "The refugia sweep's own law says loose straits make worlds stable but sterile, because connectivity homogenises the gene pool and removes the drift and bottleneck dynamics that fix new structure. That is a fair description of what the archipelago arm did here. This run cannot distinguish refugia failing at high Ne from refugia never having been tested, because the geometry drifted into the configuration the sweep already knew was sterile.",
  "properTest": "Hold lagoon size and strait width absolute and tile MORE lagoons across the larger world. That keeps local refugium structure and local Ne where they were while raising global Ne, which is the manipulation the question actually needs."
 },
 "honestLimitations": [
  "Six seeds an arm. Larger than anything before it in this project, still small.",
  "One machine, and cross machine determinism is known broken since v1.40, so these are this machine's worlds.",
  "The archipelago arm is confounded as described above and should be treated as inconclusive rather than negative.",
  "Generation matching used maxGen, the deepest lineage, which advances with the fastest breeding line rather than tracking the whole population's turnover."
 ],
 "conclusion": "Two of the project's headline claims now look different. The complete animal does not appear when selection can see, which supports the reading that the celebrated golden ages were drift excursions in very small populations. The archipelago's advantage is untested rather than disproved, and testing it properly needs a tiled geometry rather than a scaled one.",
